\begin{abstract}
We report angle-resolved photoemission spectroscopy (ARPES) evidence that the charge density wave (CDW) transition in 1\textit{T}-TiSe$_{2}$ is driven by the formation of an excitonic insulator phase. High-resolution measurements across the transition temperature $T_{\mathrm{CDW}} \simeq 200~\mathrm{K}$ reveal a pronounced reconstruction of the low-energy electronic structure, including the backfolding of Se $4p$ valence bands from $\Gamma$ to the Ti $3d$ electron pockets at $L$, the opening of a hybridization gap, and a strong temperature-dependent transfer of spectral weight. The observed band renormalization cannot be accounted for by a conventional weak-coupling Peierls instability alone, as the momentum-dependent gap and the evolution of the folded bands are consistent with electron--hole pairing between the valence-band maximum at $\Gamma$ and the conduction-band minima at $L$. In the CDW phase, the spectral function exhibits signatures of coherent excitonic hybridization, producing a reconstructed quasiparticle dispersion compatible with a $2 \times 2 \times 2$ periodic lattice modulation. The onset of these changes near $T_{\mathrm{CDW}}$ indicates that exciton condensation and lattice distortion are strongly coupled, with the excitonic order parameter providing the primary electronic instability that stabilizes the commensurate CDW. These results support the interpretation of 1\textit{T}-TiSe$_{2}$ as a candidate excitonic insulator in which Coulomb-driven electron--hole correlations play a central role in the emergence of charge order.
\end{abstract}
